+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edit Policy
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers edit a plot visit on tablets that may be offline for up to 48 hours. Two edits made from the same starting state can reach the server in either order, days apart. The record store must decide what the current visit is when such edits arrive, and that decision sets the submission envelope tablet-sync stores and sends, the shape of review work, and which record states may enter the partner's CSV. If the policy is wrong, either submitted observations are lost or coordinators face review work they cannot clear before the Friday export.
+
+The team's tenets constrain the choice: no submitted edit may be silently discarded, the path from a submitted observation to an exported value must be inspectable, and the workflow must be operable by two coordinators without a systems administrator.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **Preserves every original submission** (inherited). Passes if each accepted submission remains stored unchanged with its author and receipt time, whatever the policy does with the current state.
+- **Does not infer scientific relationships between fields** (doc-local). Passes if the server never combines two edits on the basis of field names alone. A policy that combines edits must assume that unrelated fields are independent; that assumption is the boundary this decision sets.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Preservation of the worker's submitted visit as a unit** — major. Tenet: *Do not silently discard a submitted edit.* A coordinator should be able to see one worker's complete observations as they were submitted.
+- **Explainability of the exported value** — major. Tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable.* A coordinator should be able to say which submission produced each exported value.
+- **Coordinator review workload** — major. Tenet: *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator.* Goal: *Make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export.* Review work must stay within what two people clear each week.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** — moderate. Maintained merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries are ongoing work for a small team.
+- **Readiness of visits for export** — moderate. Goal: *Export the partner's existing columns without requiring spreadsheet cleanup.* Fewer visits held back means a more complete Friday file.
+- **Submission size and tablet-side state** — minor. Whole visits are larger on each submission; base snapshots are larger on each tablet draft.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each submission carries the revision ID of the visit state the worker started from and the complete visit as the worker wants it. The server stores each accepted submission as a revision. When two submissions name the same base revision, the server keeps both as competing revisions. Neither replaces the other, and neither is merged into the other. A visit with competing revisions has no single current revision until a coordinator chooses one or records a resolution. A later submission that names a resolved revision as its base continues the visit normally.
+
+Demonstration: a visit `V-1042` is at revision `r7`. Worker A corrects an observer name and submits `r8a` with base `r7`. Worker B adds a missing species to the same visit and submits `r8b` with base `r7`. Both are stored. The visit now has two competing revisions. A coordinator compares them and resolves the visit to one of them, or to a recorded combination, before it can be exported.
+
+Analysis:
+
+- Preserves every original submission: **passes**. Each submission is stored as sent.
+- Does not infer scientific relationships: **passes**. The server never combines fields.
+- Preservation of the visit as a unit: **strong**. Each revision is a complete visit exactly as one worker submitted it.
+- Explainability of the exported value: **strong**. An exported visit points to one revision, and that revision points to its submission and author.
+- Coordinator review workload: **weak**. Any two edits from the same base produce review work, including edits to unrelated fields such as an observer name and a species addition.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **strong**. The server needs revision bookkeeping and a comparison view. It needs no field-level merge rules.
+- Readiness of visits for export: **weak**. Any visit with unresolved competing revisions is held out of the CSV until a coordinator acts.
+- Submission size and tablet-side state: **partial**. Each submission carries a full visit, and each tablet holds a base revision ID per draft.
+
+Summary: simple and faithful to what workers submitted, at the cost of more review work.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission carries its base state and the structured changes it made relative to that state. When two submissions change disjoint fields, the server combines them into one current record. When they change the same field, or fields the rules treat as related, the visit goes to review. Rules are maintained for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. For example, an observer-list edit and a species-cover edit on the same visit combine automatically under the rules as written, since they touch different fields.
+
+Demonstration: the same visit `V-1042` as above. Worker A changes the observer name and submits a change set against base `r7`. Worker B adds a species to the same visit against base `r7`. The server combines both changes into a single record `r8`. The original submissions are kept, and `r8` is recorded as derived from them.
+
+Analysis:
+
+- Preserves every original submission: **passes**. Originals are kept alongside the combined record.
+- Does not infer scientific relationships: **partial**. The rules must treat fields as independent when they are not. A species identification and its cover estimate are related even though they are separate fields, so the rules must hold them together or send them to review, and a maintained list of such relationships is required.
+- Preservation of the visit as a unit: **weak**. The current record is assembled from parts of several submissions, so no single submission is the visit a worker recorded.
+- Explainability of the exported value: **partial**. Each field can carry provenance, but an automatically combined record is explained by the rules as well as the submissions.
+- Coordinator review workload: **strong**. Disjoint edits combine without review, so the queue holds only real overlaps.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **weak**. Rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries must be written, tested, and kept current as the team's data model changes.
+- Readiness of visits for export: **strong**. Most visits with compatible edits become exportable without a coordinator.
+- Submission size and tablet-side state: **strong**. Change sets are small, and tablets keep base snapshots rather than full revisions.
+
+Summary: lower review load and faster export readiness, at the cost of maintained merge rules and combined records no single worker submitted.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The deciding criteria are the two major tenet-linked criteria that the field-aware merge weakens: preservation of the visit as a unit, and explainability of the exported value. The tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* is served by both options, but only whole-visit revisions keep each submission as the record a worker actually made, without rebuilding it from parts. The tenet *Keep the path … inspectable* is served more directly, because each exported value traces to one revision and one submission. The field-aware merge is the stronger option on review workload and export readiness. The team has accepted the larger review queue as the cost of these two properties. The choice is taken with the coordinator-workload tenet in view, and the review-export design must show how that load is managed.
+
+The option fails no gate. The field-aware merge passes the first gate and only partially passes the second, because the rules must encode relationships that the server cannot infer from field names.
+
+**What this forecloses.** The server will not combine edits from unrelated fields automatically. Every visit with competing revisions waits for a coordinator, even when the edits are compatible.
+
+**What it obligates downstream.**
+
+- Tablet-sync keeps the base revision ID for each draft. It displays pending competing revisions to the field worker and sends complete visits, not change sets.
+- Review-export provides a revision comparison screen that shows each competing revision against the others and against the last resolved revision. It holds visits with unresolved competing revisions out of the CSV. It records each resolution with the coordinator's identity and the contributing submission IDs.
+- The record store exposes, on each visit read, whether unresolved review work exists, so that tablet-sync and review-export can show it without computing it themselves.
+
+## Revisiting This Decision
+
+Re-run this judgment if either of the following holds after a full season of use:
+
+- The review queue regularly exceeds what two coordinators clear before the Friday export, and most queued visits involve edits to unrelated fields.
+- Coordinators report that comparing two whole revisions takes markedly longer than reading the changed fields, and that delay is what keeps visits out of the export.
+
+In either case, the field-aware merge is the alternative to re-examine. Its precondition is a maintained list of field relationships that the team agrees on.
